# the x86-64 calling conventions: system v and windows x64
#
# sysv64 is implemented to the system v amd64 psabi, version 1.0, chapter 3.2.3
# "Parameter Passing", as clang classifies (X86_64ABIInfo). win64 is the
# windows x64 convention (learn.microsoft.com, "x64 calling convention") as
# clang classifies it (WinX86_64ABIInfo). each row's classifier reads its
# registers from the row it is called through.
#
# sysv64 classifies a value by eightbytes: each takes the merged class of what
# lies in it, INTEGER, SSE, SSEUP, X87, X87UP or MEMORY, and a value wider than
# two eightbytes, one with a member off its alignment or one any eightbyte of
# which is MEMORY travels in memory. an INTEGER eightbyte takes the next
# integer register and an SSE one the next vector register, the SSEUP after
# it joining it there. a value whose eightbytes find too few registers of
# either class free travels whole on the stack and takes none. an X87 value
# travels on the stack as an argument, and as a result is refused, since the
# selection holds no x87 register. a result in memory travels by reference to
# a copy whose address is an implicit first argument, and the callee hands it
# back in rax. a variadic caller sets al to how many vector registers carry
# arguments.
#
# win64 gives each argument one eight byte slot by position: the first four
# take rcx, rdx, r8 and r9 or xmm0 to xmm3 by their position, the rest the
# stack at eight bytes per position, over the 32 bytes of home space the
# caller reserves. a float takes the vector register of its position and, when
# unnamed, the integer register too. a value of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes that is no
# float takes the integer register, and every other value, as a 128-bit
# integer or vector, travels by reference to a copy. a result of 1, 2, 4 or 8
# bytes comes back in rax, a float, a 128-bit vector and a 128-bit integer in
# xmm0, and any other result by reference to a copy whose address is an
# implicit first argument, handed back in rax.
#
# under sysv64 an integer narrower than 32 bits is extended as its signature
# declares, which clang assumes of its callers, and a 32-bit one leaves its
# upper half undefined. win64 extends nothing.

